it's great to be joined today by  attorney Mike Papantonio co-host of ring  of fire radio PAP DuPont and it's  spin-off company are going to be paying  921 million dollars to 3,500 plaintiffs  this is all about a toxic chemical known  as c8 which was used during the  manufacture of teflon many people know  teflon is that nonstick coating on  cooking implements how'd you first hear  about what was going on with c8 yeah  well my specialty is you probably know  David is to try cases I was brought in  as a trial lawyer to try the first first  three or four test cases before then it  had been worked up against completely  unbelievable odds the lawyers there in  the around the Ohio Valley did an  incredible job with no help from the  media no help from the government no  help from the DOJ at all they did it  single-handedly as a matter of fact  there was actually push back all along  the way by the government who simply  didn't want this story told the EPA had  really dropped the ball Department of  Justice as usual had done nothing to  investigate or prosecute the people who  were responsible so what ended up  happening is it was just a long slog  that's the best way I can I can describe  it in your research how far back was it  revealed that DuPont knew about the  health risks that were posed by c8 well  they knew about they knew about health  risks as early as the the mid 60s they  were performing tests on a lab animals  of every kind everything from monkeys  the beagles to rats and all of the tests  were bad all of the tests showed bad bad  health effects so they knew right off  the bat but by 1980s they were already  warning their own employees that c8  could cause cancer the problem was they  weren't telling the American public that  c8 could cause cancer they weren't  telling the people that were drinking  the c8 water every day that it would  cause it would cause cancer people  people were having to drink c8  contaminated water  were showering with it they were you  know preparing baby bottles with it they  were they used it every part of their  life it was it was a contamination level  that brought up the c8 blood  contamination in these people on  incredible amounts I mean  sometimes 50 times what it should have  been there should be no sea level c8 in  anybody's blood it's man-made it  shouldn't be there but it was all over  these people's lives as it is in a lot  of parts throughout this country a lot  of places in this country right here in  my hometown of Pensacola  they've got c8 in the drinking water  well it shouldn't be there it's man-made  as far as the distribution of the  plaintiffs some of some in our audience  probably know about how in Texas there  are those five-gallon office water  cooler water jugs like a thick plastic  there's pollution that comes from the  factories where those are produced that  is very very harmful to human health  carcinogenic and when you look at where  what towns are sort of mostly affected  by that pollution it's poor towns it's  disproportionately non-white towns what  kind of analysis can we make along those  lines to the 3,500 plaintiffs involved  here is there also a class story behind  it there absolutely as you hear what  happened was you had corporate America  move into the south they came in like  corporate like like corporate  carpetbaggers they knew people would  work for low wages they knew that people  wouldn't be concerned about the  environment if they were being paid  decent money because they had to make a  living they had to take care of their  family people were made willing to make  that trade-off you'd often hear here  claims you know no matter how bad the  pollution was in the South you'd always  hear some local saying well it smells  like money to me even though it was  pollution that was potentially killing  their children and so what ended up  happening was you'll find this air this  this area in the south this this Gulf  Coast area is the pollution is  extraordinary because all corporate  America knew they could come down here  pay low wages not worry about the  environment they could externalize all  of their poison all over the community  and then and then leave town when it was  all over so talk to us a little bit  about the trial I mean I'm always  fascinated  to here what case corporate defense  counsel attempts to make to distract or  reject the the allegations of liability  here what case did they try to make here  well the case was that they didn't know  in the it was how could we have known  well all they had to do is read through  documents I think the jury was moved by  the idea that every trial they would  hear the defendant stand up they'd hear  Dupont stand up in the beginning and say  gee we just didn't know if we would have  known this wouldn't happen and then  within 15 minutes in the trial they're  saying dozens and dozens of documents  that were hidden from the American  public hidden from the EPA where was  very clear that they did know in their  intent was to continue what they were  doing anyway for 50 years because they  were making so much profit on Teflon so  that's the defense and anytime you wage  a defense like that and then almost  immediately that defense is crushed by  the the documents themselves the  testimony themselves it's very very  tough to overcome  that's why juries punished on juries  felt that they showed a conscious  disregard for the health and safety of  people all along the Ohio River Valley  and so you know they got they were  getting ready to hit be hit in the  fourth trial for you know an  extraordinary amount of money but the  first trial started off with one of the  minor cases that by the way they picked  their this is the one they wanted to  test this is the one they wanted to try  and I think is 1.8 million second was  twelve point  excuse me 5.7 million third was twelve  point nine million something like that  so it just kept getting worse and worse  for them and they did what they should  have done which is to close this down  the reason it wasn't done soon enough is  because the media did not do their job  had the media done their job and told  the story that should have been told  this would have happened much sooner but  you have an American media that it that  they're ordered to do what they do by  their advertisers and if DuPont or Dow  Chemical who is getting ready to merge  with DuPont put pressure on the media  then they won't do their job we no  longer have a functional media this this  case this case is the best example of  David this case is a case that should  have gone away years ago  but the story would never be told on ABC  NBC CBS MSNBC none of the corporate  media because they simply don't do their  job  it is a worthless source of information  anymore sometimes when settlements like  this are reached there's sort of a line  in the sand Ron where there can be no  more plaintiffs or litigation going  forward is that the case here or can  there be more plaintiffs brought into  this know there's there's 70,000 people  affected by this this settlement only  has to do with about 3,000 people that  people are going to become ill as time  goes on this is this is the product that  is in their blood stays in their blood  David for 25 years it's in the  environment for 2 million years you  can't get rid of it if you can't get rid  of it anybody's blood you can't chelate  it you it's there it's going to stay  there for 25 years unfortunately as it  stays there the risk of thyroid disease  cancer just as dramatically increased  testicular cancer kidney cancer and and  so this this is what these people are  facing so going forward in this  settlement in though no lake precludes  people from being able to protect  themselves in the future well we've been  following it we're going to continue  following it we've been speaking with  attorney Mike Papantonio co-host of ring  a fire radio also host of America's  lawyer on RT always great to talk to you  thank you thank you

